# Copy to `.env` (git-ignored). The `just` recipes load it; a variable already
# set in your environment wins over the same one here. Nothing in it is needed
# if your environment already sets it. The server itself never reads `.env`:
# deployments set its DAMSON_* variables directly (docs/configuration.md).

# ─── Postgres (required) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Any Postgres 16+ where PGUSER may create databases. `just dev` creates
# damson_dev_<user> (or DEV_DATABASE) on it, and each test run creates and drops
# its own damson_test_* database. PGDATABASE is only where the recipes connect
# to create and drop those. For a throwaway local server:
#   docker run -d --name damson-pg -p 127.0.0.1:5432:5432 -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=damson postgres:16-alpine
# PGHOST=127.0.0.1
# PGPORT=5432
# PGUSER=postgres
# PGPASSWORD=damson
# PGSSLMODE=disable
# PGDATABASE=postgres
# DEV_DATABASE=

# ─── Ports (optional; defaults shown) ─────────────────────────────────────
# PORT is the API, and Vite's /api proxy follows it. WEB_PORT is Vite.
# PORT=8080
# WEB_PORT=5173

# ─── Sign-in (optional) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Unset, `just dev` signs in locally as dev@example.com / password1. Set the
# issuer and a public PKCE client id (no secret) that accepts /auth/callback on
# http://localhost:* to sign in through your identity provider instead. The
# first person to sign in becomes the sysadmin.
# DAMSON_OIDC_ISSUER_URL=https://auth.example.com
# DAMSON_OIDC_CLIENT_ID=

# ─── File storage (optional) ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# Unset, files stay under ./data. Set all five to store them in an
# S3-compatible bucket (path-style), under damson/<dev database>/.
# AWS_ENDPOINT_URL_S3=
# AWS_REGION=
# AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
# E2E_S3_BUCKET=
